      <p>
        I build everything with a tool called trunk. It runs the Rust compiler
        and generates the JavaScript that connects the program to the page.
        Even trunk had a problem. The graphics code needs one setting, and
        that setting should go in the config file of trunk. But in my version
        of trunk, the setting never reaches the compiler. So every build
        script sets it as an environment variable instead.
      </p>

      <h2>It compiled, but it did not run</h2>
      <p>
        When I compiled the code for the browser, I found the problems that I
        expected. There is no system trash and no clipboard. There is no
        source of random numbers until you tell a library to use the one in
        the browser. And starting a thread fails. These problems appear at
        compile time or right at startup, and they were easy to fix.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I opened the app in a browser, and it crashed.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Asking for the time crashed the app.</strong> The standard
          Rust function <code>Instant::now()</code> has no implementation in
          the browser. It crashes instead of returning an error. The app reads
          the clock at startup, so every launch died. Now every timer in the
          app uses a small library that asks the clock of the browser. A
          patched copy of the RAW decoder uses it too.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>I could not wait on the main thread.</strong> The desktop
          app waits for the graphics card to be ready before it shows a
          window. A browser page must give control back to the browser, or
          nothing happens. Now graphics setup starts in the background. The
          app checks on every frame whether the setup is done.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The window started at zero by zero.</strong> The canvas is
          0&times;0 pixels until the browser reports its size. WebGPU, the
          browser's way to use the graphics card, rejects every drawing
          command outside the canvas. On a high-resolution screen, the
          rectangles that I use to clip drawing were also twice too big.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>WebGPU is stricter than Metal.</strong> One shader stopped
          early for some pixels and then read from a texture. (A shader is a
          small program that runs on the graphics card.) Metal, the graphics
          system from Apple, and Vulkan allowed that without a warning. WebGPU
          refused to compile it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The colours were wrong</h2>
      <p>
        When the app first drew something, everything looked dull and dark.
        A 50% gray appeared as approximately 21%.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the desktop, the app gives colours to the graphics card in linear
        light. Linear values scale with the real amount of light. The system
        then converts them to sRGB, the values that a monitor expects. A
        WebGPU canvas never offers that conversion. So the linear values went
        straight to the screen, and every midtone was far too dark. Gray test
        bars that should have read 64, 128 and 192 read 13, 55 and 134.
      </p>
      <p>
        I fixed it: the shaders now do the conversion themselves, on every
        platform, including the desktop. This last step is now shared, so the
        desktop and browser builds cannot drift apart in how colour reaches
        the screen. Decoding is still different. On macOS, Apple's ImageIO
        decodes the photos. On all other platforms, the app's own decoders do
        it.
      </p>

      <h2>Threads without shared memory</h2>
      <p>
        Before I started the port, I wrote a memo on whether it was possible
        at all. For threading, the memo predicted the usual approach. That
        approach uses memory that the workers share, and the site must send
        two special security headers. It also needs a library that lets the
        Rust thread pool run in the browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        That library did not fit. It expects JavaScript to set up the thread
        pool before the Rust program starts. But LightPhotos is a normal Rust
        program with its own <code>main()</code>. So I dropped shared memory
        completely. Each worker runs its own separate copy of the worker
        program. The main thread sends it the bytes of a file and gets pixels
        back.
      </p>
      <p>
        The result was simpler than my plan. It needs no special headers from
        the server and no experimental Rust. The cost is memory, because each
        worker has its own. That is why the pool stops at four workers, no
        matter how many cores you have.
      </p>
      <p>
        But the memo was right about memory. A browser tab can only address a
        limited amount of memory, because it is 32-bit. And when that memory
        grows, it never shrinks again. I wrote about this in <a
        href="/blogs/measuring-a-gui-app.html">measuring a GUI app</a> and
        <a href="/blogs/never-load-the-whole-folder.html">never load the
        whole folder</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>No recovery after a crash</h2>
      <p>
        On the desktop, if the RAW decoder panics on an unusual file, the app
        catches the panic and shows a placeholder. The browser build cannot
        do that. WebAssembly in the browser has no way to recover from a
        panic, so the panic kills the worker outright.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the app checks each file before it gives the file to the decoder:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>The app rejects file layouts that the decoder does not implement yet, where the decoder would hit a <code>todo!()</code>.</li>
        <li>The app rejects files from Fujifilm X-Trans sensors, because they hit an <code>unreachable!()</code> in the decoder.</li>
        <li>
          The app checks image sizes for overflow. In the 32-bit browser
          build, the width times the height of a big RAW file can overflow.
          That never happens on a 64-bit Mac.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When it can, the worker skips the decoder completely. It uses the
        JPEG preview that most cameras embed in the RAW file. That is faster,
        and it cannot crash.
      </p>

      <h2>Files, one handle at a time</h2>
      <p>
        The File System Access API is what makes the web version possible.
        You pick a folder, and the page can read and write inside it. But the
        page never sees a path, only handles. And every operation is
        asynchronous: you ask, and the answer arrives later. Code that said
        "list the directory" now says "ask for the list, and use it when it
        arrives".
      </p>
      <p>
        Two errors took me the longest time to understand.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong><code>NotReadableError</code>.</strong> Chrome throws it
          when too many file reads run at once. Two at a time never caused it.
          The app allows four. When a read fails, the app tries again after a
          random delay that grows each time. When I retried right away, or at
          fixed intervals, the reads failed again. The cause was that all the
          reads retried at the same moment.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>RangeError: Array buffer allocation failed</code>.</strong>
          When the app read a RAW file, it copied the file once into
          JavaScript and again into the memory of WebAssembly. After a few of
          those reads in a row, the tab ran out of memory. Now the bytes move
          across without the extra copy.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I have not fixed some rough edges yet. You must pick the folder again
        every time you open the page. When you delete a photo, the deletion is
        permanent, because the API has no trash. The app warns you about that
        before it deletes.
      </p>

      <h2>Deploying broke it</h2>
      <p>
        The worst bug in the whole port was not in the Rust code. Cloudflare
        serves the site. It cached the JavaScript of the worker for four hours
        and its WebAssembly file for zero. After a release, recent visitors
        got old JavaScript paired with new WebAssembly. Every decode then
        failed with an error that said a function did not exist.
      </p>
      <p>
        To fix it, the app now adds the build hash of the main bundle to the
        end of the worker file addresses. (The build hash is a fingerprint of
        that build.) So every release gets new addresses for both files. When
        I moved the app under <code>/app/</code>, the worker address broke
        too. Now the worker finds its own location from the page and does not
        assume it.
      </p>

      <h2>What the browser version gives up</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>It works only in desktop Chrome and Edge, which have WebGPU and folder access.</li>
        <li>No HEIC, no face detection and no subject selection.</li>
        <li>No Immich upload, because Immich servers do not accept requests from web pages.</li>
        <li>No clipboard and no Lightroom preset import. You also cannot choose where exports go. They go to an <code>Exports</code> folder inside the folder that you picked.</li>
        <li>You download approximately 4.3&nbsp;MB, compressed, before the app starts.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Was it worth it?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes, I think so. Sending someone a link is much easier than asking
        them to install something. The port also made the desktop app better.
        Every platform now uses the same shader colour conversion. Thread
        pools no longer panic when they cannot start. And the thumbnail cache
        now lives next to your photos.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you try the <a href="/app.html">web app</a> and it breaks, I would
        really like to know which browser you used and what you were doing.
        The <a href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/issues">issue
        tracker</a> is the best place to tell me.
      </p>

